---
/**
 * Politique de signature du code, exigée par la SignPath Foundation (certificat gratuit des projets open source) :
 * ce qui est signé et comment, l'équipe et ses rôles, la confidentialité, la mention de SignPath. Liée depuis le pied
 * de page (donc l'accueil) et la page de téléchargement. `SIGNED_BUILDS.windows` passe à vrai quand la signature est
 * en place : le statut de la page suit.
 */
import LegalDoc from "@/layouts/LegalDoc.astro";
import { LINKS } from "@/lib/site";
import { SIGNED_BUILDS } from "@/lib/release";

const WORKFLOW = `${LINKS.github}/blob/main/.github/workflows/desktop-release.yml`;
---

<LegalDoc
  title="Code signing policy"
  description="How TaskForce Desktop for Windows is built and signed: from the public source code, by GitHub Actions, with a certificate by the SignPath Foundation."
  updated="20 October 2026"
>
  <h2>Signing</h2>
  {
    SIGNED_BUILDS.windows ? (
      <p>
        Free code signing provided by <a href="https://signpath.io">SignPath.io</a>, certificate by{" "}
        <a href="https://signpath.org">SignPath Foundation</a>.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        We are setting up free code signing provided by <a href="https://signpath.io">SignPath.io</a>, certificate by{" "}
        <a href="https://signpath.org">SignPath Foundation</a>, for TaskForce Desktop for Windows. Until it is in place, the
        Windows installer is not signed, and Windows or your antivirus may warn you before running it.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2>What we sign</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The TaskForce Desktop installer for Windows (<code>TaskForce-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code>), published on the{" "}
      <a href="/download">download page</a> and in the project's GitHub releases.
    </li>
    <li>
      It is built from the public source code of the <code>main</code> branch by the{" "}
      <a href={WORKFLOW}>Desktop - Release</a> workflow on GitHub Actions. We sign nothing built anywhere else, and only
      binaries built from this repository.
    </li>
    <li>
      The app bundles open source components from other projects (Electron, a Java runtime, PostgreSQL). They keep
      their own publishers' signatures, or none: we do not sign them in their name.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Team and roles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Committers and reviewers:</strong> Pierre Michel (<a href="https://github.com/Miche1-Pierre">Miche1-Pierre</a>).
      Changes from anyone outside the team come as pull requests, reviewed by a committer before they are merged.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Approvers:</strong> Pierre Michel. Every signing request is approved by hand, release by release.
    </li>
    <li>Team members must use two-factor authentication on GitHub and on SignPath.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    What TaskForce Desktop sends, and to whom, is described in our <a href="/legal/privacy">privacy policy</a>: it signs
    you in to your TaskForce account, asks GitHub whether a new version exists, and sends data to the AI providers and
    services you choose to connect. It sends us no usage data.
  </p>

  <h2>Check a download</h2>
  <p>
    Each file of a release shows its SHA-256 digest on its <a href={`${LINKS.github}/releases`}>GitHub release page</a>.
    On Windows, compare it with <code>Get-FileHash TaskForce-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code> in PowerShell.
  </p>
</LegalDoc>
